    "narrative": "The Austin Waves offensive engine today is anchored by **Gunnar Henderson**, who earns our highest efficiency score of 81.88. His matchup against Severino is prime territory; the combination of a favorable hitter\u2019s park and the pitcher\u2019s middling 4.60 xERA allows **Gunnar Henderson** to capitalize on his elite leadoff position. Similarly, **Kyle Tucker** (69.90) and **Jacob Wilson** (69.51) represent our strongest high-floor options. **Jacob Wilson** benefits significantly from a +16% park factor adjustment, while **Luis Garc\u00eda Jr.** (67.25) leverages a strong platoon advantage against Alcantara, helping to offset a minor BvP headwind. We are prioritizing these bats to aggressively chase counting stats, particularly in the HR and RBI columns, while relying on the speed upside inherent in our top-of-order hitters.\n\nThe most difficult exclusion today was **Nasim Nunez**, who posted a respectable 53.72 efficiency score but ultimately couldn't crack the starting nine. While **Nasim Nunez** benefits from a slow-delivery pitcher who is vulnerable to the stolen base\u2014a key factor for our roster construction\u2014his anchor at the #9 spot significantly suppresses his run-production ceiling compared to the primary lineup. We also continue to monitor the status of **Luis Robert Jr.**, who remains sidelined on the IL, limiting our depth in the outfield rotation. \n\nManagers should be wary of the matchup for **Sam Antonacci**; despite a strong individual xwOBA, he faces an elite arm in Gilbert (3.09 xERA), which creates a high-variance scenario. Additionally, while **Heliot Ramos** carries a sub-50 score, he remains active due to favorable wind conditions blowing out, which provides a latent power upside not fully captured by his raw projection. With no further injury updates, we are set to roll with this configuration, focusing on the high-probability opportunities presented by our middle-order hitters against Elder and Alcantara.",
